@node Graph and Network Analysis
@chapter Graph and Network Analysis
@cindex graph
@cindex digraph
@cindex network
@cindex adjacency matrix

Octave provides classes for representing and analyzing graphs, also
called networks.  An undirected graph is represented by a @code{graph}
object, a directed graph by a @code{digraph} object, and the plotted
representation returned by @code{plot} is a @code{GraphPlot} handle
object.  The API follows MATLAB's @code{graph} and @code{digraph}
classes introduced in R2015b so that scripts written against the MATLAB
documentation run unchanged.

Graphs are stored internally as sparse adjacency matrices (@pxref{Sparse
Matrices}) so Octave can handle networks with millions of nodes and
edges efficiently, and operations that accept sparse matrices, such as
eigenvalue decompositions or linear solves, apply directly to the
adjacency.

The following short example constructs a simple undirected graph on
five nodes with six edges, queries a few basic properties, computes
the unweighted shortest-path distances from node 1, and draws the
graph:

@example
@group
s = [1 1 2 3 3 4];
t = [2 3 4 4 5 5];
G = graph (s, t);
numnodes (G)              # => 5
numedges (G)              # => 6
d = distances (G, 1)      # => [0 1 1 2 2]
p = plot (G);             # returns a GraphPlot handle
@end group
@end example

The same graph can equivalently be constructed from an adjacency matrix
or from an edge table.  Every construction form preserves sparse input.
Weighted edges, named nodes, and both 2-D and 3-D layouts are supported.

