@node Graph Objects
@section Graph Objects

The @code{graph} and @code{digraph} classes are value classes, so every
structural mutation returns a new object and leaves the original
unchanged.  They share a common set of methods; the @code{digraph}
class additionally distinguishes incoming from outgoing edges and
supports directed-only operations such as @code{isdag} and
@code{toposort}.

A @code{graph} or @code{digraph} may be constructed from pairs of
integer or string endpoints, from a numeric or sparse adjacency
matrix, or from an @code{EdgeTable} / @code{NodeTable} pair of struct
arguments.  An optional cellstr of node names makes every query and
result refer to the names rather than the numeric indices.  Edge
weights, when supplied, are carried through every operation that
preserves the edge set.

@DOCSTRING(graph)

@DOCSTRING(digraph)

@DOCSTRING(GraphPlot)

@node Graph Structure and Queries
@section Graph Structure and Queries

The following functions return basic structural properties of a graph
without modifying it.  They accept both @code{graph} and @code{digraph}
objects unless otherwise noted.  Queries that distinguish edge
direction (@code{indegree}, @code{outdegree}, @code{predecessors},
@code{successors}, @code{inedges}, @code{outedges}) are only meaningful
on @code{digraph} objects; on an undirected @code{graph} object the
@code{indegree} and @code{outdegree} values coincide with
@code{degree}, and @code{predecessors} / @code{successors} both equal
@code{neighbors}.

@DOCSTRING(numnodes)

@DOCSTRING(numedges)

@DOCSTRING(edgecount)

@DOCSTRING(findnode)

@DOCSTRING(findedge)

@DOCSTRING(degree)

@DOCSTRING(indegree)

@DOCSTRING(outdegree)

@DOCSTRING(neighbors)

@DOCSTRING(predecessors)

@DOCSTRING(successors)

@DOCSTRING(inedges)

@DOCSTRING(outedges)

@DOCSTRING(ismultigraph)

@node Modifying Graphs
@section Modifying Graphs

Because @code{graph} and @code{digraph} are value classes, the
functions in this section do not modify their input in place; they
return a new object reflecting the change.  Nodes and edges can be
added or removed individually, in bulk, or by name; the edge set can
be restricted or re-indexed; self-loops and parallel edges can be
collapsed; and a subset of nodes can be extracted as a standalone
graph.

@DOCSTRING(addnode)

@DOCSTRING(addedge)

@DOCSTRING(rmnode)

@DOCSTRING(rmedge)

@DOCSTRING(flipedge)

@DOCSTRING(reordernodes)

@DOCSTRING(subgraph)

@DOCSTRING(simplify)

@node Matrix Representations of Graphs
@section Matrix Representations of Graphs

A graph can be converted to a numeric matrix representation suitable
for standard linear-algebra operations.  The adjacency matrix gives
the direct connectivity, the incidence matrix maps edges to their
endpoints, and the Laplacian is used for spectral clustering and
embedding.  All three functions return sparse matrices by default and
honor edge weights when present.

@DOCSTRING(adjacency)

@DOCSTRING(incidence)

@DOCSTRING(laplacian)

@node Graph Traversal and Shortest Paths
@section Graph Traversal and Shortest Paths

Traversal functions visit nodes in breadth-first or depth-first order
starting from a source, optionally returning a discovery-event log
rich enough to reproduce tree edges, back edges, and cross edges.
Shortest-path functions return either a single source/target pair, a
single-source shortest-path tree, or the full pairwise distance matrix,
and automatically select an algorithm (Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford, Johnson,
DAG topological, or unweighted BFS) appropriate for the edge-weight
sign and graph structure.  Enumeration helpers return all simple paths
or all simple cycles in a graph.

@DOCSTRING(bfsearch)

@DOCSTRING(dfsearch)

@DOCSTRING(shortestpath)

@DOCSTRING(shortestpathtree)

@DOCSTRING(distances)

@DOCSTRING(allpaths)

@DOCSTRING(allcycles)

@DOCSTRING(isdag)

@DOCSTRING(toposort)

@node Connectivity and Flow
@section Connectivity and Flow

Connectivity functions partition the node set into connected (for
@code{graph}) or weakly/strongly connected (for @code{digraph})
components.  The condensation of a digraph collapses each strongly
connected component to a single node, producing an acyclic summary
graph.  Transitive closure and reduction produce the smallest and
largest graphs with the same reachability as the input digraph.  The
maximum-flow and minimum-cut functions compute integral flows on a
capacitated network using Edmonds-Karp or Boykov-Kolmogorov search
trees.

@DOCSTRING(conncomp)

@DOCSTRING(biconncomp)

@DOCSTRING(condensation)

@DOCSTRING(transclosure)

@DOCSTRING(transreduction)

@DOCSTRING(maxflow)

@DOCSTRING(mincut)

@node Centrality Measures
@section Centrality Measures

The @code{centrality} function computes node importance scores using
a user-selectable measure.  Supported measures include
@qcode{"degree"}, @qcode{"indegree"}, @qcode{"outdegree"},
@qcode{"closeness"}, @qcode{"incloseness"}, @qcode{"outcloseness"},
@qcode{"betweenness"}, @qcode{"pagerank"}, @qcode{"eigenvector"},
@qcode{"hubs"}, and @qcode{"authorities"}.  Edge weights are accepted
through the optional @qcode{"Cost"} (shortest-path length) and
@qcode{"Importance"} (spring-weight) parameters so the same measure
can be evaluated under several different weighting conventions.

@DOCSTRING(centrality)

@node Graph Isomorphism
@section Graph Isomorphism

Isomorphism testing and enumeration are implemented with the VF2
backtracking algorithm.  Attribute matching on nodes and edges is
supported through the @qcode{"NodeVariables"} and
@qcode{"EdgeVariables"} options, letting graphs with user-defined
labels be compared up to any desired equivalence.

@DOCSTRING(isisomorphic)

@DOCSTRING(isomorphism)

@node Graph Visualization
@section Graph Visualization

Visualization of a graph or digraph is driven by the @code{plot}
method, which returns a @code{GraphPlot} handle.  The handle exposes
the placed coordinates (@code{XData}, @code{YData}, and @code{ZData}
for 3-D layouts) and cosmetic properties such as @code{NodeColor},
@code{NodeLabel}, @code{MarkerSize}, @code{EdgeColor}, @code{EdgeAlpha},
@code{LineStyle}, @code{LineWidth}, and @code{ArrowSize}.  Supported
layouts include @qcode{"auto"} (the default, which falls back to
spectral on small graphs and force-directed on larger ones),
@qcode{"circle"}, @qcode{"force"}, @qcode{"force3"}, @qcode{"layered"},
@qcode{"subspace"}, and @qcode{"subspace3"}.  The @code{highlight},
@code{labelnode}, and @code{labeledge} functions modify an existing
@code{GraphPlot} in place to emphasize a subset of nodes or edges,
without re-running the layout.

@example
@group
G = graph ([1 1 2 3 3 4], [2 3 4 4 5 5]);
p = plot (G, "Layout", "force");
highlight (p, [1 2 3]);         # emphasize a subset
labelnode (p, 1:numnodes (G), @{"A", "B", "C", "D", "E"@});
@end group
@end example

@DOCSTRING(highlight)

@DOCSTRING(labelnode)

@DOCSTRING(labeledge)
